    "summary": "Epic Weather Report Suite → Dark Star chain, jazzy second‑set brilliance",
    "blurb": "The Tampa show shines with a seamless Weather Report Suite → Dark Star → Eyes of the World flow and a fiery China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider segue. Jerry’s soaring solos and Keith’s lush keys drive a jazz‑infused second set that builds to a volcanic Wharf Rat and Let It Grow. The soundboard sources are crystal‑clear, making this one of ’73’s standout performances.",
    "review": "December 18, 1973 at Curtis Hixon Convention Hall captured the Dead at a creative peak. After a solid first set anchored by Tennessee Jed and a tight China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider, the second set erupted into a legendary sequence: Weather Report Suite Prelude → Part 1 → Let It Grow → Dark Star → Drums → Eyes of the World → Wharf Rat → Sugar Magnolia. Jerry Garcia’s slide‑like tone on the Weather Report Suite and his incendiary solo through Dark Star are among his most inspired moments, while Phil Lesh’s crunchy, low‑register bass underpins the entire jam marathon. Keith Godchaux’s piano and organ add a subtle genius that colors the jazzy passages, and Bill Kreutzmann stays perfectly in the pocket, especially during the drum break into Eyes of the World. The show closes with a soaring Let It Grow and a heartfelt Uncle John’s Band encore, cementing this concert as a quintessential snapshot of the band’s late‑’70s jazz‑rock apex.",
